  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe employ a mixed discrete-continuous optimization approach to minimize the overlap integral C\u2085 for the Erd\u0151s conjecture. The core idea is to iteratively refine the step function h using a combination of alternating optimization and simulated annealing to escape local minima. Starting from the provided `initial_h_values`, we randomly perturb the current solution, project it onto the feasible region, and accept improvements. This balances exploration and exploitation, leveraging the initial construction while avoiding local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^(n_points), with sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Objective: min_h(max_k Corr(h, 1\u2212h, k) * dx)\n- Feasibility projection: Clip h[i] to [0,1], then rescale to meet sum constraint\n- Update rule: h_new = h_old + \u03b1\u0394h, where \u0394h is a random perturbation\n- Annealing schedule: Temperature T decreases over time to reduce randomness\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\nInitialize h = initial_h_values if provided, else random starting point with sum(h) = n_points / 2 and h[i] \u2208 [0,1].\n\nb. Precompute constants:\n\u0394x = 2.0 / n_points, overlap_lags = 2*n_points - 1\n\nc. Main loop:\nWhile time budget allows:\n- Compute current Corr(h, 1-h) and C\u2085\n- Generate random perturbation \u0394h with magnitude scaled by current temperature\n- Update h_candidate = h + \u03b1*\u0394h\n- Project h_candidate onto feasible region:\n   i. Clip each element to [0,1]\n   ii. Compute current sum, adjust elements to meet sum(n_points / 2)\n- Compute new C\u2085 for h_candidate\n- If new C\u2085 < current best C\u2085 or exp((current C\u2085 - new C\u2085)/T) > random(0,1):\n   Accept h_candidate\n- Decrease temperature according to schedule\n\nd. Early stopping:\nIf no improvement for 100 iterations, terminate early\n\ne. Return best result:\nAfter time budget exhausted, return best h and its C\u2085\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial points:\n  - Use `initial_h_values` as starting point if available\n  - Else, initialize h with uniform random samples clipped and scaled\n- Parameters:\n  - \u03b1 = 0.1 (step size)\n  - T starts at 0.1, decreases by 0.99 every 10 iterations\n  - n_points = 200 (trade-off between accuracy and speed)\n- Perturbation distributions:\n  - Uniform random in [-0.1, 0.1] per element initially\n  - Shrinking window as T decreases\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTotal steps: ~1000 iterations at 1 sec each (1000 sec), with early exit\nMemory: O(n_points) for h and working storage\nRobustness:\n- Check feasibility after each projection\n- Handle floating point errors with tolerance 1e-6\n- Validity checks on Corr computation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Assert 0 <= h[i] <=1 and abs(sum(h) - n_points/2) < 1e-6\n- Recompute C\u2085 using eval Corinthians to verify",
